+## Session 2026-09-08 — Q54 P12.4 C4b CUDA Arena Ownership
+
+C4b turns the separately reviewed C2 render and C3 scene/LBVH stages into one
+reusable CUDA allocation graph without making the transaction itself
+resettable. The arena owns a validated template per slot/frame, but every
+prepared sample receives a fresh dormant single-use transaction. Only an
+arena-issued C4 lease can activate that sample for enqueue, and the same slot
+event cannot be submitted again until exact reclaim advances the generation.
+
+The resource manifest distinguishes physical allocations from logical tensor
+views. This matters for transform replay: multiple frame views intentionally
+share one underlying Torch allocation, so recording only the first view's
+shape would give a false ownership proof. Each physical record now carries all
+logical names, dtypes, shapes, strides, active byte offsets, and active byte
+ranges, while total bytes count the allocation once.
+
+C3 provenance is not accepted as three caller-supplied hashes. A benchmark-side
+adapter constructs the canonical C3 one-shot binding from the arena's actual
+stage objects and tensors. The arena canonicalizes it and checks row identity,
+operation and dispatch evidence, pointer, dtype, shape, active byte range, and
+owner identity before issuing a bounded provenance capability. Production
+`optics` code does not import the benchmark package; the dependency remains in
+the allowed benchmark-to-product direction.
+
+R0 retains the entire prepared packed-scene allocation set plus the entire
+derived fixed-fixture set, even though the render kernel consumes only a
+subset. That preserves the approved a2 provenance and upload ownership chain
+instead of silently redefining the fixed workload at the C4 boundary. R1 uses
+the dynamic CUDA rebuild class, while R2 keeps its separately frozen static
+class even though both currently select the same CUDA LBVH implementation.
+
+Device results are exposed through a generation-scoped view. Access requires
+terminal resolution and fails after release/reclaim. C4b intentionally does
+not add host staging, async readback, ordinary close/poison cleanup, C0 guardian
+handoff, result activation, or baseline production; those remain C4d/B3/joint
+review work.
+
+The first C4b review exposed three distinctions that an internally consistent
+arena graph did not yet prove. First, hiding an executable under an underscore
+is not authority: an arena-owned template, its stages, and its terminal event
+must all reject activation or execution unless a live C4 lease supplies the
+registered capability. Standalone C2 and arena-controlled C2 therefore need
+different activation policy even though they share the same transaction state
+machine.
+
+Second, matching hashes and storage signatures do not prove an allocation came
+from a2. The durable proof now combines exact live prepared/readiness identity,
+exact tensor or approved-view identity, an untimed content revalidation, and an
+adapter-issued receipt identity. Serialization can reproduce facts but cannot
+reproduce the authority showing who observed those facts.
+
+Third, "allocation-free" is weaker than "constant-time." A measured acquire
+that walks every sealed slot, frame, owner, and resource record can remain free
+of Torch allocations while still scaling with the workload. Full graph
+validation belongs in seal/prepare; acquire should consume only fixed identity
+records and the C4 slot/generation state transition.
+
+A follow-up review showed that an opaque token is not automatically a live
+capability. The first correction still let the private executable present its
+static template token as execution authority, so the token proved graph
+membership but not lease ownership. Construction and execution must use
+different authorities: the former may create dormant state, while the latter
+must be minted only after acquire and bind the transaction to the exact live
+lease, slot, generation, and lifecycle state.
+
+The stronger formulation couples authorization with lifecycle transitions.
+The live authority begins C4 enqueue before exposing stage execution and
+records C4 completion immediately after the terminal event. This preserves the
+invariant even if internal objects are reached directly: there is no successful
+terminal submission whose lifecycle receipt still describes a free or
+completion-free slot.
+
+That coupling is still insufficient if the timeline authenticates its
+controller with module/name strings and callable discovery. Python objects can
+impersonate all three without owning any lifecycle state. The final boundary
+therefore uses a timeline-defined sealed exact type plus bidirectional identity
+registries tying authority, transaction, and the real `C4ActiveOutputLease`.
+The bind API accepts a lease, not a caller-constructed authority; the timeline
+mints the only registered authority itself.
+
+Pre-submit rejection also needs an explicit disposition. If authority identity
+fails before `begin_enqueue`, no GPU command is in flight, so cancelling the
+lease and burning the generation is safe. Treating that case like a submitted
+failure would neither poison the control arena nor release the still-`LEASED`
+slot, creating a fail-closed-looking resource leak.
+
+Exact authority type and registry membership are necessary but not sufficient
+when the mint operation itself remains reusable. A real lease can still be
+misapplied if static construction authority may mint repeatedly or if the
+caller chooses an empty binding. The final C4b correction therefore makes
+execution authority a two-step linear capability: untimed preparation freezes
+one exact transaction/owner/binding, and successful measured acquire consumes
+that capability while registering the real lease exactly once.
+
+The transaction's slot, frame, and terminal-event identity must be sealed on
+the template before any dormant activation. C3 digests then extend that fixed
+identity during preparation; they do not replace it. This prevents two
+transactions that share shapes, stages, or even one slot event from exchanging
+leases. It also gives the mint API no generic binding collection to omit or
+rewrite.
+
+Finally, "owner looks active" is not an ownership proof. The timeline rejects
+duck-typed owners and retains the exact active object in both prepared and live
+registries. Authorization rechecks the same owner, transaction, authority, and
+lease identities on every enqueue and finish boundary.
